= Practice Questions (Exam Pattern)

Ten questions designed in authentic CUET UG single-correct style. The official marking scheme awards $+5$ for each correct answer and deducts $-1$ for every incorrect answer.

#mcq(1, [Globalisation fundamentally deals with interconnected 'flows' across borders. Which of the following is NOT one of the four core flows?], (
  [Flow of Ideas], [Flow of Capital], [Flow of Commodities], [Flow of Sovereign Territory],
))

#mcq(2, [In political terminology, the reduction of state activities to core functions such as maintaining law, order, and national security is referred to as:], (
  [The Totalitarian State], [The Minimal State], [The Welfare State], [The Feudal State],
))

#mcq(3, [The worldwide expansion of standardized Western fast-food chains, consumer brands, and lifestyles is popularly analyzed as:], (
  [Cultural Heterogenisation], [Cultural Homogenisation (McDonaldization)], [Cultural Relativism], [Cultural Pluralism],
))

#mcq(4, [The creative blending of external cultural elements with local traditions (such as wearing a khadi kurta over blue jeans) is termed:], (
  [Cultural Homogenisation], [Cultural Imperialism], [Cultural Heterogenisation], [Ethnocentrism],
))

#mcq(5, [In which city did massive street protests by 50,000 civil society activists shut down the WTO Ministerial Conference in November 1999?], (
  [Geneva], [Seattle], [Davos], [Cancun],
))

#mcq(6, [The World Social Forum (WSF) was established in 2001 under which famous rallying slogan?], (
  [Workers of the World Unite], [Another World is Possible], [Free Trade for All], [Make In India],
))

#mcq(7, [In which country was the first inaugural summit of the World Social Forum (WSF) convened in January 2001?], (
  [Porto Alegre, Brazil], [Mumbai, India], [Geneva, Switzerland], [Nairobi, Kenya],
))

#mcq(8, [The fourth meeting of the World Social Forum (WSF), which mobilized massive participation from Indian civil society, took place in which city in 2004?], (
  [New Delhi], [Kolkata], [Mumbai], [Bengaluru],
))

#mcq(9, [In which year did India formally launch structural economic adjustment reforms under the New Economic Policy (LPG)?], (
  [1977], [1985], [1991], [1998],
))

#mcq(10, [Indian civil society and legal authorities famously challenged and overturned foreign patents granted on which indigenous botanical resources?], (
  [Wheat, Rice, and Barley], [Neem, Turmeric, and Basmati Rice], [Tea, Coffee, and Rubber], [Sugarcane, Mustard, and Cotton],
))

= Detailed Solutions

#solution(1, [Globalisation involves the transnational movement of ideas, capital, commodities, and people. Sovereign territory does not flow; state physical boundaries remain legally fixed.], answer: [D])

#solution(2, [Under economic globalization, states often retreat from welfare functions to become a 'minimal state' focused primarily on defending borders and enforcing basic contracts and law.], answer: [B])

#solution(3, [Cultural homogenisation, also termed 'McDonaldization', describes the global spread of uniform Western consumer culture and commercial products threatening local cultures.], answer: [B])

#solution(4, [Cultural heterogenisation (or cultural hybridity) describes the creative process whereby external influences are modified and fused with indigenous cultural practices.], answer: [C])

#solution(5, [In November 1999, the 'Battle of Seattle' erupted when 50,000 protesters blocked and disrupted the 3rd Ministerial Conference of the WTO in Seattle, USA.], answer: [B])

#solution(6, [The World Social Forum (WSF) was founded under the global slogan 'Another World is Possible', advocating an alternative, democratic, people-centered globalisation.], answer: [B])

#solution(7, [The inaugural World Social Forum was held in January 2001 in Porto Alegre, Brazil, as a civil-society counterweight to the corporate World Economic Forum in Davos.], answer: [A])

#solution(8, [The 4th World Social Forum met in Mumbai, India, in January 2004, marking the first time the global conclave was hosted outside Brazil.], answer: [C])

#solution(9, [In July 1991, the Narasimha Rao government launched the New Economic Policy (LPG), dismantling industrial licensing and opening the Indian economy to foreign capital.], answer: [C])

#solution(10, [India successfully fought biopiracy battles in international patent offices, revoking or modifying foreign commercial patents granted on Neem, Turmeric, and Basmati rice.], answer: [B])

